  "# BIP-021: Light-Node Authority, Session Recovery, Browser Parity & `:443` Passthrough Resilience (Stage 0 Proposal)\n\n| Field | Value |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **BIP Number** | `BIP-021` |\n| **Pipeline Stage** | **Stage 0 Proposal** (Stage 0(a) telemetry-triggered hardening + Stage 0(b) browser/updater parity) |\n| **Proposer** | `Nexus` (on behalf of `Simon`, `Axiom`, `Vector`, `Lumen`) |\n| **Base Commit (`HEAD`)** | `b072e6a` |\n| **Change Class** | **Staging-First** (`bitecho-gentoo-lab`, per `RELEASE.md` §3 — spans Core Network / Transport / Broadcast, Relay Passthrough off-heap lifecycle, and Rollout / Auto-Update mechanics) |\n| **Stage 2 Ack Window** | `N = 10,000` epochs, **directed-delivery clock start** (never broadcast-only expiration), unanimous **5/5** real circle consent (`simon`, `axiom`, `nexus`, `vector`, `lumen`) |\n| **Canonical Artifact Path** | `bitecho.org/dev` → `dist/bips/bip-021-proposal.md` |\n| **Pre-Implementation Guard** | **Zero BIP-021 code on `main` before Real Stage 2 Draft Consent** (`docs/GOVERNANCE.md` §2) |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Motivation & Ground-Truth Audit (`HEAD b072e6a`)\n\nProduction and devnet operation on `b072e6a` surfaced six concrete failure modes spanning light-node startup authority, direct-envelope subnet routing, DAC vs. signature diagnostics, post-restore session `tx-seq` rollback blackholes, browser parity gaps, and `:443` passthrough / auto-update resilience. Every item below has been ground-truth verified against `HEAD` (`b072e6a`) and adheres strictly to `AGENTS.md` Rules 1–6 (Strict TDD, Sans-IO pure core, Clean CI, Single-Authority shell lifecycle state, Zero Silent Failures, Co-Committed Spec/Docs/Ops/Flag Inventory).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Technical Specification\n\n### 2.1 Startup `subnet_id` Authority (No Sticky Persisted Override)\n\n#### Ground-Truth Defect (`b072e6a`)\n- **`src/bitecho/node_main.cljs`** (daemon `L4142–4159`, one-shot `L4609–4626`) and **`src/bitecho/shell/light_node.clj`** (daemon `L3372–3389`, one-shot `L3842–3859`): when `loaded-state` is loaded from `state.fressian`, `(cond-> loaded-state ...)` does **not** apply `(:subnet-id opts)`. `(:subnet-id opts)` is only passed to `light-core/init-light-state` (`src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc:485`) on a cold start when `loaded-state` is `nil`. Consequently, a stale persisted `:subnet-id` silently overrides an explicit `--subnet-id` CLI flag on restart.\n- **`src/bitecho/browser_main.cljs`** (`init-node` `L609–637`, `apply-restored-state!` `L230–269`): `(merge curr clean-loaded)` in `apply-restored-state!` unconditionally overwrites `:subnet-id` and `[:light-state :subnet-id]` with whatever stale `:subnet-id` was persisted in IndexedDB, even when the caller explicitly passed `initNode({subnetId})`.\n\n#### Specification\n1. Add a pure Sans-IO core function in `src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc`:\n   ```clojure\n   (reconcile-startup-subnet-id state explicit-subnet-id) -> {:state new-state :effects effects}\n   ```\n   - When `explicit-subnet-id` is blank/`nil`, return `state` unchanged with no effects (persisted `:subnet-id` remains authoritative when the operator did not pass an explicit override).\n   - When `explicit-subnet-id` is provided and `(not= (str (:subnet-id state)) (str explicit-subnet-id))`:\n     - Set `:subnet-id` to `(str explicit-subnet-id)`.\n     - Ensure `:subscribed-subnets` includes `(str explicit-subnet-id)` (preserving existing subscriptions).\n     - Update `:network-uri` if `explicit-subnet-id` maps to a canonical network URI in `config/network-uris`.\n     - Increment `[:diagnostics :subnet-id-reconciled]` (Rule 5 counted diagnostic).\n     - Emit a structured `:diagnose` effect / stdout event:\n       `{\"event\" \"subnet-id-reconciled\" \"from\" old-subnet \"to\" new-subnet}`.\n2. Wire `reconcile-startup-subnet-id` into all four startup/restore paths:\n   - `src/bitecho/node_main.cljs` (daemon & one-shot).\n   - `src/bitecho/shell/light_node.clj` (daemon & one-shot).\n   - `src/bitecho/browser_main.cljs`: record `:explicit-subnet-id` in `node-atom` when `opts` supplies `:subnet-id` / `:subnetId`, and invoke `reconcile-startup-subnet-id` inside `apply-restored-state!` so IndexedDB restore never clobbers an explicit startup `subnetId`.\n\n#### Invariant (TDD / `test.check`)\n- For any persisted state with `:subnet-id S1` and any explicit non-blank startup `S2 != S1`, `(:subnet-id new-state) == S2`, `(contains? (effective-subscribed-subnets new-state) S2)`, and `[:diagnostics :subnet-id-reconciled]` increments by exactly `1`.\n- When `explicit-subnet-id` is `nil` or equals `S1`, `[:diagnostics :subnet-id-reconciled]` is unchanged (`0` spurious diagnostics).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Subscribed-Subnet Admission for Direct Envelopes\n\n#### Ground-Truth Defect (`b072e6a`)\n- **`src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc`** (`handle-wire-payload`, `L9290–9303`):\n  - For `:p2p-broadcast`, `sub-bcast?` admits envelopes whose `eff-inner` is in `sub-set` (`(effective-subscribed-subnets state)`) or equivalent to `expected-subnet`.\n  - For non-broadcast direct envelopes (`(not storage-or-cap?)`, lines `L9299–9303`), `subnet-mismatch?` checks **only** `(config/equivalent-subnet-id? outer-subnet expected-subnet active-net-uri)` and `(config/equivalent-subnet-id? inner-subnet expected-subnet active-net-uri)`, ignoring `(:subscribed-subnets state)` completely. Direct P2P offers, answers, sealed messages, and acks tagged with a subscribed subnet other than the primary `:subnet-id` are dropped with `subnet-mismatch-dropped`.\n\n#### Specification\n1. Define a pure helper `subnet-admitted?` in `src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc`:\n   - Returns `true` if `candidate-subnet` is `nil` or `\"*\"`, or `(= expected-subnet \"*\")`, or `(contains? sub-set \"*\")`, or `(config/equivalent-subnet-id? candidate-subnet expected-subnet active-net-uri)`, or `(some #(config/equivalent-subnet-id? candidate-subnet % active-net-uri) sub-set)`.\n2. Preserve the strict inner-vs-outer consistency check (`outer-subnet` and `inner-subnet`, when both present and non-`\"*\"`, MUST be equivalent via `config/equivalent-subnet-id?`).\n3. For non-broadcast, non-`storage-or-cap?` envelopes, admit when both `outer-subnet` and `inner-subnet` pass `subnet-admitted?` against `(effective-subscribed-subnets state)`.\n4. Unsubscribed subnets continue to be rejected loudly with `:subnet-mismatch-dropped` and `(record-suppression :subnet-mismatch)`.\n\n#### Invariant (TDD / `test.check`)\n- Direct envelopes tagged with any subnet in `(effective-subscribed-subnets state)` (or equivalent via `config/equivalent-subnet-id?`) are never dropped as `:subnet-mismatch`.\n- Direct envelopes with mismatched `outer-subnet` vs. `inner-subnet`, or tagged with a subnet absent from `(effective-subscribed-subnets state)`, are always rejected and increment `:subnet-mismatch-dropped`.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Distinct `:dac-required-missing` / `:broadcast-dac-*` Diagnostics\n\n#### Ground-Truth Defect (`b072e6a`)\n- **`src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc`** (`L8636–8661`):\n  `sig-valid?` (`L8636`) and `dac-valid?` (`L8641`) are conflated in a single branch `(if-not (and sig-valid? dac-valid?) ...)` (`L8656`) that only increments `[:diagnostics :broadcast-rejected]` and records `:admission-drop`, with `:stdout-events []`. Operators cannot distinguish a forged/corrupted broadcast signature from a validly signed broadcast rejected because DAC gating is active (`requires-dac?`) and the sender omitted or supplied an invalid/untrusted DAC.\n\n#### Specification\nSplit broadcast admission failure in `src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc` into three distinct, mutually exclusive reasons (evaluated in strict order: signature first, then DAC presence, then DAC validity), while preserving the aggregate `[:diagnostics :broadcast-rejected]` counter for backward compatibility:\n\n| Failure Condition | Counted Diagnostic Key | Suppression Key | Structured Diagnostic Event `\"reason\"` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `(not sig-valid?)` | `[:diagnostics :broadcast-sig-invalid]` | `:broadcast-sig-invalid` | `\"broadcast-sig-invalid\"` |\n| `sig-valid?` ∧ `requires-dac?` ∧ `(not (map? wire-dac))` | `[:diagnostics :broadcast-dac-missing]` | `:dac-required-missing` | `\"dac-required-missing\"` |\n| `sig-valid?` ∧ `(map? wire-dac)` ∧ `(not dac-valid?)` | `[:diagnostics :broadcast-dac-invalid]` | `:broadcast-dac-invalid` | `\"broadcast-dac-invalid\"` |\n\nEach rejection emits a structured Rule 5 diagnostic event `{\"event\" \"broadcast-rejected\" \"reason\" <reason> \"sender\" norm-sender \"subnet_id\" sub-id \"seq\" seq-num ...}` (including `\"dac_error\"` when `verify-dac` returns an error or `\"untrusted-anchor\"`).\n\n#### Invariant (TDD / `test.check`)\n- Missing-DAC rejection with a valid Ed25519 signature increments `:broadcast-dac-missing` and `:broadcast-rejected`, and **never** increments `:broadcast-sig-invalid` or `:broadcast-dac-invalid`.\n- Invalid-signature rejection increments `:broadcast-sig-invalid` and `:broadcast-rejected`, regardless of DAC presence.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4 Session `tx-seq` Rollback Detection & Recovery\n\n#### Ground-Truth Defect (`b072e6a`)\n- **Incident Motivation:** After a VM snapshot restore, Lumen's persisted `state.fressian` rolled back a session's local `:tx-seq` to `18` while the remote peer's `:rx-high-seq` was `20`.\n- **Receiver side (`src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc:7647–7660`):** When `sealed/check-and-advance-replay-window` returns `{:error :replayed-seq}`, the receiver decrypts the message, returns a signed `session-ack` carrying its `contiguous-rx-seq` and `seen-seqs`, increments `[:sessions session-id :replays-deduped]`, and emits `:stdout-events []`.\n- **Sender side (`src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc:7667–7717`):** `handle-wire-session-ack` updates `:acked-seq` via `(max (:acked-seq session 0) ack-seq)` and purges outbox entries `<= ack-seq`, but **never compares `ack-seq` / `(apply max 0 seen-seqs)` against local `(:tx-seq session)`**. As a result, messages `19` and `20` are silently blackholed as `replays-deduped` (and worse, reusing a rolled-back `tx-seq` under the same session AEAD key is a **nonce-reuse hazard**).\n\n#### Specification (Zero Weakening of Replay Protection)\n1. **Receiver-side invariant (unchanged security boundary):** The receiver **NEVER** decrements `:rx-high-seq` or delivers a `:replayed-seq` ciphertext to `:inbox`. On `:replayed-seq`, in addition to incrementing `[:sessions session-id :replays-deduped]` and returning the signed `session-ack`, the receiver increments `[:diagnostics :session-replay-detected]` and emits a structured Rule 5 diagnostic event:\n   `{\"event\" \"session-replay-detected\" \"session_id\" sid \"sender\" sender \"seq\" msg-seq \"rx_high_seq\" rx-high-seq \"replays_deduped\" n}`.\n2. **Sender-side rollback detection & safe fast-forward in `handle-wire-session-ack`:**\n   - Compute `peer-high-seq = (long (reduce max (max ack-seq acked-seq) seen-vec))` from the **Ed25519-verified** `session-ack` signed by `(:peer session)`.\n   - If `(> peer-high-seq (long (:tx-seq session 0)))`:\n     - Increment `[:diagnostics :session-tx-seq-rollback-detected]`.\n     - Emit a loud structured Rule 5 event:\n       ```json\n       {\n         \"event\": \"session-seq-mismatch\",\n         \"session_id\": \"<sid>\",\n         \"peer\": \"<peer>\",\n         \"local_tx_seq\": 18,\n         \"peer_high_seq\": 20,\n         \"reconciled_tx_seq\": 20,\n         \"reoffer_recommended\": true\n       }\n       ```\n     - Atomically advance `[:sessions session-id :tx-seq]` to `peer-high-seq` so the very next outbound message uses `(inc peer-high-seq)` (`21`), strictly preventing further AEAD nonce collision and blackholing, while setting `:reoffer-recommended? true` on the session so operators/UI can cleanly rotate session keys via `p2p-offer`.\n     - **Do not** silently drop messages that were queued locally at rolled-back seqs `<= peer-high-seq` without surfacing them: mark affected outbox entries as `:rollback-collision` and emit a counted `:session-tx-seq-rollback-evicted` diagnostic (or re-notify the caller via `\"session-seq-mismatch\"` with `\"affected_seqs\"`).\n3. **Monotonic `tx-seq` persistence hardening:** In `node_main.cljs` and `shell/light_node.clj`, persist `state.fressian` atomically (write-temp + `fsync` + rename) **before** flushing outbound sealed envelopes on `send`, minimizing crash/snapshot-rollback windows.\n\n#### Invariant (TDD / `test.check`)\n- A verified `session-ack` with `peer-high-seq > local-tx-seq` always advances `(:tx-seq session)` to `peer-high-seq` and emits `session-seq-mismatch`; an unverified/forged `session-ack` never mutates `:tx-seq`.\n- Across arbitrary VM-restore rollback traces (`tx-seq` reset to `k < rx-high-seq`), the receiver never delivers a replayed ciphertext, and after a single `session-ack` round-trip, the sender's subsequent message is accepted and delivered with `seq = peer-high-seq + 1`.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5 Browser Parity (`src/bitecho/browser_main.cljs`, `src/bitecho/shell/browser.cljc`, & Companion `alpeware/hive`)\n\n#### Ground-Truth Defects (`b072e6a`)\n1. **Disconnected `ctl(\"status\")`:** `src/bitecho/shell/browser.cljc:1617–1621` (`exec-browser-cli`) delegates directly to `light-node/handle-command` on `(:light-state state)`, which reads `(:connected? light-state false)` (`light_node/core.cljc:4963,4971`). Live WebRTC mesh connectivity lives in `(:mesh-conns state)` (`browser_main.cljs:847` `:meshConnected`) and is never mirrored into `ctl(\"status\")`, causing `\"connected\": false, \"transport_healthy\": false` even with 6/6 WebRTC peers open.\n2. **Missing periodic `auto-sync`:** `src/bitecho/browser_main.cljs:1430–1449` only calls `fetch-relay-chain-state!` inside the 2s redial interval when `zero-open?` is `true`. When the WebRTC mesh is connected, no periodic background `auto-sync` refreshes `finalized_epoch`, causing epoch drift and `epoch-skew-dropped`.\n3. **Local-only plaintext Rolodex:** `src/bitecho/light_node/core.cljc:7066–7128` (`exec-rolodex-put` / `exec-rolodex-restore`) only stores plaintext snapshots in local memory (`:net-envelopes []`), so YubiKey recovery on a new device or after an IndexedDB reset cannot restore petnames or subscribed subnets.\n\n#### Specification\n1. **Single-Authority `ctl(\"status\")` Liveness Projection (Rule 4):**\n   - Never persist `:connected?` or `:connection-error` to IndexedDB.\n   - In pure `browser/project-light-state-liveness` (called inside `exec-browser-cli`), derive `:connected?` from `(or (pos? (count (:open-peers mesh-summary))) (boolean (:relay-fallback-healthy? state)))` and `:connection-error` from `(:last-error mesh-summary)` (only when not connected), plus expose `\"mesh_open_peers\"` and `\"relay_fallback_active\"` in `ctl(\"status\")`.\n2. **Bounded Periodic Browser `auto-sync`:**\n   - Add a pure generation-tagged `auto-sync` scheduler reducer in `src/bitecho/shell/browser.cljc`:\n     - Active interval: `30,000 ms` when tab is `\"visible\"` and either `meshConnected` or relay fallback is active.\n     - Hidden tab policy: pause or throttle to `>= 180,000 ms` when `document.visibilityState === \"hidden\"`, and fire one immediate catch-up sync on `hidden -> visible` transition.\n     - Single in-flight guard (`:auto-sync-in-flight?`) so slow networks never pile up overlapping requests.\n     - Feeds every sync result into `light-core/evaluate-auto-sync-stall` (Rule 5) and updates `finalized_epoch` cleanly across both mesh `chain-state-query` and HTTPS relay fallback.\n3. **Encrypted On-Network `rolodex-put` / `rolodex-restore`:**\n   - Derive a deterministic 32-byte Rolodex AEAD key from the user's identity secret via domain-separated HKDF (`\"bitecho/rolodex-v1\"` + `pubkey-hex`).\n   - Encrypt the canonical secret-free `bitecho/rolodex-snapshot-v1` (petnames, `petname-meta`, `subscribed-subnets`, monotonic `:seq`; **zero private keys**) into a `bitecho/encrypted-rolodex-v1` envelope (`{:schema \"bitecho/encrypted-rolodex-v1\" :pubkey ... :seq ... :nonce-hex ... :ciphertext-hex ...}`).\n   - Publish ciphertext bytes as a content-addressed blob (`SHA-256 == CID`) via `artifact-put` and update a signed monotonic-`:seq` pointer at deterministic path `rolodex/<pubkey-hex>/latest.enc` on `bitecho.org/rolodex` (or configured home subnet).\n   - `rolodex-restore` fetches the pointer + blob (mesh-first with HTTPS fallback), verifies pointer signature and `SHA-256(bytes) == target-cid`, decrypts using the unlocked identity key, rejects downgrade (`remote-seq < local-seq` unless `--force`), and merges petnames + subscribed subnets into state.\n   - **Companion `alpeware/hive`:** On YubiKey unlock, automatically invoke `rolodex-restore` when local IndexedDB is empty or behind remote `:seq`; trigger debounced `rolodex-put` after petname or subnet-subscription changes.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6 Auto-Update & `:443` Passthrough Resilience\n\n#### Ground-Truth Defects (`b072e6a`)\n1. **Premature `:destroy-socket` on clean `peer-fin?` (`src/bitecho/core/infra.cljc:824–850`):**\n   In `transition-passthrough-pair`, `:socket-close` computes `peer-fin?` (`L825–826`) and then line `L836` checks `(or (boolean (:had-error? event)) peer-fin?)`, immediately transitioning to `:closed` and emitting `{:effect :destroy-socket :side peer}`. When the backend finishes writing a TLS response + `close_notify` and closes cleanly while the client socket still has unread/unflushed bytes in flight, destroying the peer socket sends a premature TCP `RST` and truncates in-flight TLS/HTTP responses.\n2. **HTTPS-only `:fetch-bundle-blob` (`src/bitecho/node_main.cljs:4032–4048`, `src/bitecho/shell/updater.clj:110–119`):**\n   Both Node.js and JVM auto-updaters unconditionally fetch release blobs over HTTPS (`deploy/build-blob-fetch-url`), never attempting mesh-first `artifact-get` as mandated by `RELEASE.md` §5.\n\n#### Specification\n1. **`:443` Passthrough Graceful Drain (`src/bitecho/core/infra.cljc`):**\n   - In `transition-passthrough-pair` for `:socket-close`:\n     - If `peer-closed?`: transition to `:closed`, clear drain timer.\n     - Else if `(boolean (:had-error? event))`: transition to `:closed`, clear drain timer, emit `{:effect :destroy-socket :side peer}`.\n     - Else (including when `peer-fin?` is `true`!): transition to `:draining` (`:drain-timer-active? true`), emit `{:effect :end-socket :side peer}` and `{:effect :schedule-drain-timeout :side peer :timeout-ms (:drain-timeout-ms st1)}` (default `5000 ms`).\n   - Only `:had-error?`, `:socket-error`, or `:drain-timeout` may emit `:destroy-socket` on a non-closed peer.\n   - Extend model-based `clojure.test.check` command-sequence tests for `transition-passthrough-pair` to verify (a) zero premature `:destroy-socket` on clean `peer-fin? true`, (b) bounded cleanup within `5000 ms` on stalled peers (zero leaked `TCPSocketWrap` handles / `ArrayBuffer` slabs), and (c) stale-generation callbacks emit counted `:stale-passthrough-callback`.\n2. **Mesh-First `:fetch-bundle-blob` with HTTPS Fallback (`src/bitecho/node_main.cljs` & `src/bitecho/shell/updater.clj`):**\n   - When executing `:fetch-bundle-blob` for `{:cid cid :generation gen}`:\n     1. If mesh transport is connected, attempt mesh `artifact-get` for `cid` with a bounded timeout (`15,000 ms`).\n     2. Verify `SHA-256(bytes) == cid`. On match, dispatch `{:type :blob-fetched :cid cid :bytes bytes :source :mesh :generation gen}`.\n     3. On mesh unconnected, timeout, not-found, or hash mismatch, emit a counted Rule 5 diagnostic event `{\"event\" \"auto-update-diagnose\" \"reason\" \"auto-update-mesh-fallback\" \"cid\" cid \"mesh_error\" err \"fallback\" \"https\"}` and fall back automatically to HTTPS `deploy/build-blob-fetch-url` (`30,000 ms` timeout), verifying `SHA-256(bytes) == cid` before dispatching `:blob-fetched` with `:source :https`.\n   - Preserve monotonic pointer `:seq` gate and autonomous client LKG self-fallback (`RELEASE.md` §5).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Governance, Stage 1–3 Gates & Stage 4 Soak Declaration\n\n### 3.1 Governance Parameters (`docs/GOVERNANCE.md` §2)\n- **Stage 1 (Strictly Advisory):** Independent Blind Round-1 Twin Runner with isolated frontier-model calls per seat and mandatory falsifiable `:falsifier` slot (`\"what breaks in prod\"`). Twin output is strictly advisory and never authorizes implementation.\n- **Stage 2 Gate 1 (Draft Consent):** Publish Stage 1-shaped `dist/bips/bip-021-DRAFT.edn` on `bitecho.org/dev`, verify 3/3 HTTP 200 + SHA-256 across `ns1..ns3`, open directed-delivery consent round with **`N = 10,000` epochs** ack window (clock starts only on directed-session delivery, never broadcast alone), require **5/5 unanimous real circle consent** before any BIP-021 code is written on `main`.\n- **Stage 3 (Strict TDD):** Every fix lands with its failing `clojure.test.check` / in-memory reproducer first (Rule 1), passes `clojure -M:format`, `clojure -M:lint`, `clojure -M:test`, `clojure -M:test-cljs`, and publishes an 8-gate Stage 2 receipt + self-contained `.patch` on `bitecho.org/dev` before tagging or rollout.\n\n### 3.2 Stage 4 Staging Soak & Chrome Raw-CDP E2E Pass/Fail Criteria\n\n**Declared Soak Class:** **Staging-First** (`bitecho-gentoo-lab`, per `RELEASE.md` §3). Because §2.6 modifies `:443` passthrough socket teardown and off-heap `ArrayBuffer` drain lifecycle, this BIP **MUST** pass a duration-bounded `P-MEM-1` + `P-FAIL-1` staging soak before live cluster rollout.\n\n| Gate | Pass Criterion (All Required — Fail-Closed) |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **1. `P-MEM-1` Staging Soak Duration (`bitecho-gentoo-lab`)** | Continuous **`>= 60 minutes`** under active `:443` passthrough + mesh load. Post-GC `/statusz` `{rss, external, arrayBuffers}` slope **`<= +5%`** after 10-min warm-up; **`0`** leaked `TCPSocketWrap` handles. |\n| **2. `:443` Passthrough Half-Close E2E** | `>= 1,000` TLS `:443` passthrough connections exercising clean backend `peer-fin? true` half-close: **`0`** premature TCP `RST` / `ECONNRESET` truncations; **`100%`** stalled-peer drains reclaimed within `5,000 ms ± 500 ms`. |\n| **3. Cluster Quorum & Artifact Parity (`P-FAIL-1`)** | `6/6` staging validators online, **`0`** `finalized_epoch` stalls (`F-EPOCH-1`), **`3/3`** HTTP 200 + `SHA-256 == CID` parity across `ns1..ns3`. |\n| **4. Light-Node Populated-State & Rollback E2E (Node.js + JVM)** | (a) Restart with explicit `--subnet-id` overrides stale persisted `:subnet-id` and records `subnet_id_reconciled = 1`. (b) Direct envelope on subscribed subnet admitted (`subnet_mismatch_dropped = 0`). (c) Validly signed broadcast missing required DAC increments `broadcast_dac_missing = 1` with `broadcast_sig_invalid = 0`. (d) Simulated VM-restore rollback (`tx-seq: 18 <= rx-high-seq: 20`) rejects replays on receiver, emits `session-seq-mismatch` on sender, advances `tx-seq` to `20`, and delivers `seq = 21` cleanly. |\n| **5. Mesh-First Auto-Update Failover E2E (Node.js + JVM)** | (a) Connected mesh hit fetches bundle via `artifact-get` with `0` HTTPS blob requests and `SHA-256 == CID`. (b) Simulated mesh miss emits `auto-update-mesh-fallback` and succeeds via HTTPS fallback. (c) Corrupted/failing candidate triggers autonomous LKG fallback with `0` boot-loops. |\n| **6. Chrome Raw-CDP Headless Browser E2E** | (a) `initNode({subnetId: \"bitecho.org/dev\"})` over stale IndexedDB `:subnet-id \"global\"` reconciles to `\"bitecho.org/dev\"` (`subnet_id_reconciled = 1`). (b) `ctl(\"status\")` reports `\"connected\": true` and `\"transport_healthy\": true` when `meshConnected === true`. (c) Background `auto-sync` advances `finalized_epoch` across `>= 2` intervals (`<= 65s`) with `epoch_skew_dropped = 0`, and pauses/throttles when `visibilityState === \"hidden\"`. (d) `rolodex-put` → wipe IndexedDB → `rolodex-restore` recovers `100%` of petnames & subscribed subnets with `SHA-256 == CID` verified and `0` plaintext petnames present in the on-network blob bytes. |