    "BIP-021 (CID 1618fbd593514d65ae33d136e887dbd13e4c27851825fafad364968b09d1d7f1, audited against b072e6a spec and verified primitives in local 74d760f) addresses genuine operational defects, but as specified introduces blocking cryptographic, trust-boundary, liveness, and AGENTS.md Rules 1-6 violations across sections 2.1-2.6:\n\n1. S2.1 Startup authority vs. persisted state: Switching --subnet-id across canonical networks (e.g., testnet -> mainnet) updates :network-uri without fencing or resetting prior-network state (:account/:finalized-epoch poisoning min-finalized-epoch in handle-wire-chain-state-snapshot, :chain-state-snapshots, :sessions, :relay-since-broadcast-seq, or stale :subscribed-subnets). In browser_main.cljs, :subnet-id is duplicated across top-level :subnet-id, :conn, and :light-state (violating Rule 4 Single-Authority); meanwhile hive/resources/trace_viz/chat.js unconditionally calls initNode({subnetId: \"devnet.bitecho.org\"}) on load (L1688, L2161), so treating JS app defaults as explicit --subnet-id CLI authority would overwrite user-persisted network choices on reload unless explicit overrides are distinguished from defaults.\n\n2. S2.2 Direct-envelope subnet admission: Making subnet-admitted? return true whenever candidate-subnet is nil or \"*\" opens a wildcard/untagged spoofing bypass. Combined with unwrap-wire-message (core.cljc:6497) copying outer :subnet-id onto untagged inner maps and outer relay wrappers sending \"*\" (L2456, L6776, L7429), an attacker can wrap an unsolicited channel-offer or rendezvous-offer-header with outer-subnet=\"*\" and inner-subnet=nil or \"*\" from an unsubscribed subnet; the inner-vs-outer check only runs when both are non-\"*\", so the offer enters :pending-offers and can auto-accept when :autonomous? is true. Furthermore, config/equivalent-subnet-id? (config.cljc:105-115) returns true whenever two IDs are both in active-roots, conflating distinct canonical roots, while exec-send (core.cljc:3226) stamps (:subnet-id state) instead of (:subnet-id session), causing asymmetric drops across S2.1 overrides unless open sessions authorize frames by cryptographic session-id + network binding and silent drops emit counted :diagnose (Rule 5).\n\n3. S2.3 DAC vs. signature separation: Ordering sig-valid? before DAC checks prevents unauthenticated verify-dac amplification, but handle-wire-broadcast (core.cljc:7836-7842) skips rejection if requires-dac? is false and wire-dac is present yet non-map/malformed. All malformed early returns and catch paths must emit record-suppression + counted :diagnose (Rule 5), and new event types (broadcast-rejected, subnet-id-reconciled, session-seq-mismatch, session-replay-detected) must be registered in actionable-event-types and projected in exec-status.\n\n4. S2.4 AEAD nonce reuse, rollback recovery, & deadlock: CRITICAL FLAW. seq->nonce (sealed.cljc:786-797) deterministically derives nonces from tx-seq. Because tx-seq rollback is only detected upon receiving session-ack, exec-send has ALREADY emitted a second ciphertext under the same (tx-key, seq->nonce(seq)), leaking P1 XOR P2 and breaking AES-256-GCM GHASH key H. Simply bumping :tx-seq to (inc peer-high-seq) keeps a compromised :tx-key active, and advising operators to rotate via p2p-offer fails because handle-wire-p2p-offer/answer (core.cljc:7308-7650) only exchange ICE/transport candidates and NEVER rotate :tx-key/:rx-key. Second, check-and-advance-replay-window (sealed.cljc:1029-1065, window-size 64) returns :replayed-seq only when drift < 64 and :stale-seq otherwise; handle-wire-sealed-msg (core.cljc:6847-6861) drops :stale-seq silently with zero session-ack, creating permanent deadlock for rollbacks >= 64 and violating Rule 5. Third, handle-wire-session-ack (core.cljc:6895-6906) must mark collided outbox entries <= peer-high-seq as :rollback-collision BEFORE (acked? s) purges them, and peer-high-seq must be bounded (<= 2^53-1) against max-jump/overflow DoS. Fourth, synchronous fsync inside serve-mode send violates non-blocking shell patterns unless modeled as an async write-barrier before flushing net-envelopes (and still cannot stop VM snapshot rollbacks).\n\n5. S2.5 Rolodex HKDF, WebAuthn PRF, & infra authorization: HKDF from \"user's identity secret\" fails in browser for webcrypto-non-extractable keys (:extractable? false, usages [\"sign\" \"verify\"] in webauthn.cljc:485-517) and on fresh-device YubiKey login because build-assertion-options (webauthn.cljc:219-235) omits :prf when :seed-bytes is nil. Cross-runtime HKDF also fails interoperability without normalizing IKM (32-byte raw seed via extract-ed25519-seed32 vs 48-byte PKCS#8 DER in webauthn.cljc:676-712) and pubkey-hex (lowercase 88-char SPKI), specifying AEAD cipher + AAD (schema || pubkey || be64(seq)), and using random 96-bit nonces rather than deterministic (key, seq) nonces that collide under --force or multi-device races. Operationally, bitecho.org/rolodex does not exist in core/infra.cljc (bitecho.org/infra is :allowlist and verify-pointer enforces :pointer-subnet-mismatch while core/subnet.cljc forbids permissionless :streamlet-bft), and exec-artifact-put (core.cljc:5305-5324) synthesizes a fallback-kp when locked instead of failing closed! BIP-021 also diverges from existing BIP-016 rolodex-put/rolodex-restore in memento/rolodex.cljc and fails Rule 6 by omitting --force from known-command-flags and skill/SKILL.md and splitting changes across hive.\n\n6. S2.6 :443 passthrough & mesh-first updater: Passthrough :draining lacks an explicit {:effect :cancel-drain-timeout} on :closed (leaking timers/socket refs), lacks counted :diagnose on :drain-timeout (Rule 5), leaves half-open readable traffic on the live side unspecified, and has no bounded draining-pair/byte cap against Slowloris exhaustion. In the updater, moving mesh+timeout+HTTPS loops into shell imperatively violates Rules 2 & 4; reduce-light-update-tick (deploy/core.cljc:1632-1697) fails to guard :poll-tick/:pointer-seen during :phase :fetching, causing self-cancelling livelock whenever --auto-update-interval-ms <= 45000ms (15s mesh + 30s HTTPS). Critically, verify-config-pointer (deploy/core.cljc:321) fails OPEN when :trusted-deploy-authorities is empty and node_main.cljs (L3588-3595) omits it, enabling untrusted mesh/HTTPS bundle injection.\n\n7. AGENTS.md Rules 1-6 & Section 4 Open Questions:\n- Rules 1-6: Requires test.check command-sequence models (including stale-generation timers, >=64 rollback, wildcard outer envelopes, WebAuthn PRF/non-extractable failure cases, updater :poll-tick reentrancy), pure Sans-IO updater/auto-sync/passthrough reducers (Rule 2/4), zero silent drops via counted :diagnose (Rule 5), and co-committed SPEC/memento-continuity-spec/SKILL.md/known-command-flags tests + pinned hive CID (Rule 6).\n- Open Question 1 (tx-seq rollback): Mandatory automatic X25519 rekey handshake + quarantine of compromised :tx-key, NOT bump-only and NOT p2p-offer. Sending a second frame under reused (tx-key, nonce) has already broken AES-256-GCM confidentiality and GHASH integrity; bump-only continues traffic on a compromised key, and p2p-offer does not rotate :tx-key/:rx-key. On rollback detection, mark collided outbox entries :rollback-collision, trigger real key rotation (channel-offer/accept or dedicated session-rekey with :tx-rollback), re-encrypt collided messages under the fresh key, and emit session-ack for :stale-seq (>=64 gap) rather than dropping silently.\n- Open Question 2 (Rolodex HKDF & infra policy): Versioned KEK derivation: normalize raw Ed25519 to 32-byte seed for \"hkdf-sha256-seed32\", and use WebAuthn PRF with fixed deterministic domain salt \"bitecho/rolodex-prf-v1\" (or explicit user recovery passphrase fallback, never reading :extractable? false keys) under \"webauthn-prf-v1\". Unify with BIP-016 memento/rolodex.cljc, use AES-256-GCM with AAD=(schema, normalized-88char-spki-pubkey, be64(seq)) and CSPRNG 96-bit nonces, fail closed when identity is locked (remove fallback-kp in exec-artifact-put), and deploy bitecho.org/rolodex as :gate/mode :permissionless, :gate/admission :open, :replication/pointers :gossip-crdt with \"rolodex/<pubkey>/*\" -> :self-path-owner ACL (data.cljc:742-752) and per-pubkey byte quota (<= 256 KiB).\n- Open Question 3 (Mesh-first 15s/30s vs reentrancy & memory): Do NOT run a monolithic 15s mesh fetch or imperative shell fallback. Use a pure Sans-IO 2-phase updater reducer (:fetching-mesh -> :fetching-https -> :idle) with a short peer probe/first-byte timeout (2500-3000 ms), read-progress idle cap, strict max-bundle-bytes OOM bound, fail-closed :trusted-deploy-authorities + SHA-256 digest match against signed pointer manifest, and guard :poll-tick/:pointer-seen while fetching (buffer latest verified pointer or emit counted :diagnose :update-poll-suppressed).",
