   {:critique
    "From an operator reliability, incident postmortem, and unattended edge-node resilience perspective, BIP-021 diagnoses six real pain points in b072e6a but contains critical cryptographic, state-reconciliation, and livelock defects that will still silently lose messages, mislead operators, or wedge unattended nodes in production. (1) Sec 2.4 tx-seq Rollback & Open Question 1: Checking (> peer-high-seq (:tx-seq session)) in handle-wire-session-ack fails on the most common VM-restore cases because exec-send increments local :tx-seq BEFORE the envelope leaves the node. On a 1-message rollback (pre-snapshot tx-seq=19, restored=18, sender calls send once -> local tx-seq=19) or any burst of N >= (peer_rx_high_seq - restored_tx_seq) sends before the first session-ack returns, local (:tx-seq session) == peer-high-seq, so (> peer-high-seq local-tx-seq) is false: zero rollback diagnostics fire, the post-restore messages are silently dropped by the receiver as :replayed-seq and falsely purged from the sender's :outbox as acked, and AEAD nonce reuse occurs silently. Worse, Sec 2.4 recommends rotating keys via p2p-offer (:reoffer-recommended? true), which is a false remedy: p2p-offer in light_node/core.cljc only negotiates transport candidates and keeps the exact same :tx-key/:rx-key and session-id. Under AES-256-GCM (or ChaCha20-Poly1305) nonce reuse has already occurred on the colliding seqs before session-ack arrives (breaking GCM GHASH authenticity for all future seqs 21+). Furthermore, Sec 2.4 ignores symmetric/receiver rollback (when a VM restore rolls back :rx-high-seq as well as :tx-seq, causing silent permanent inbox gaps between restored rx-high-seq and peer tx-seq and duplicate delivery of unacked peer outbox entries), ignores that one-shot run-one-shot! in node_main.cljs and shell/light_node.clj persists state.fressian AFTER network send (and write-bytes-atomic-async! lacks fsync, while browser IndexedDB persist-state! runs fire-and-forget after route-browser-envelope!), and forgets that fsync cannot survive hypervisor VM snapshots. For Open Question 1: neither blind auto-advance alone nor permanent manual freeze on unattended nodes is safe—fast-forward :tx-seq only as a nonce-fence, bind session-ack to stored 64-slot ciphertext-sha256 digests to catch (== peer-high-seq local-tx-seq) collisions, taint the session, freeze AES-GCM/non-autonomous sends while auto-initiating a real cryptographic offer/rendezvous-offer (NOT p2p-offer) in :autonomous? mode, surface evicted post-restore messages in status and actionable event queues, and emit a reconnect/startup session-sync probe before send. (2) Sec 2.1/2.2 Subnet Migration & Admission: Reconciling :subnet-id on restart does not reconcile --subscribe-subnets/--subnets on loaded-state, leaves stale cross-network :sessions/:account/:relay-since-broadcast-seq when switching network URIs, drops the prior primary subnet S1 from effective-subscribed-subnets unless preserved, ignores --subnet-id when run-one-shot! delegates to a running daemon over /ctl, and risks browser dual-authority drift between top-level :subnet-id/[:conn :subnet-id] and [:light-state :subnet-id]. In Sec 2.2, exec-send, build-session-ack-envelope, and handle-wire-p2p-offer tag replies with (:subnet-id state) rather than the session/incoming subnet, causing peers on secondary subscribed subnets to drop replies with subnet-mismatch-dropped; and :chain-state-snapshot is not in storage-or-cap?, risking dropped quorum syncs when primary :subnet-id is a non-root subnet like bitecho.org/dev. (3) Sec 2.3 DAC Diagnostics: Malformed broadcast drops remain silent, exec-status only exposes :conn-diagnostics (hiding [:diagnostics :broadcast-dac-missing], :subnet-id-reconciled, :session-tx-seq-rollback-detected, and :session-replay-detected from ctl status), and DAC rejection events omit local_epoch, dac_id, and anchor_pubkey needed to separate stale epoch sync from untrusted anchors. (4) Sec 2.5 Browser Parity, Rolodex & Open Question 2: Open Question 2 must use dedicated zero-burn bitecho.org/rolodex (network-URI-isolated, self-sovereign path ACL :pointer/authority == path-pubkey, with --rolodex-subnet override for airgapped labs), NOT the primary home subnet, to avoid a chicken-and-egg recovery deadlock on blank devices that do not yet know their subscribed subnets. However, Sec 2.5.3 reinvents an incompatible schema conflicting with shipped BIP-016 (memento/rolodex.cljc grow-only revocations), fails to normalize identity secrets to canonical 32-byte seed32 (breaking cross-runtime decryption across 48-byte JVM PKCS#8, JS, and non-extractable WebCrypto), omits AEAD nonce uniqueness/AAD spec (risking nonce reuse on concurrent multi-device seq=1 writes), omits :pointer/authority == unlocked-pubkey verification, and performs naive map-merge on YubiKey unlock that resurrects deleted petnames/unsubscribed subnets or overwrites offline edits. Meanwhile, browser auto-sync lacks a bounded per-tick timeout (wedging :auto-sync-in-flight? forever on sleep/hung WebRTC) and deadlocks offline recovery when both meshConnected and :relay-fallback-healthy? start false. (5) Sec 2.6 Passthrough, Auto-Updater & Open Question 3: Fixed 5000ms :draining timeout on :443 passthrough will still RST-truncate multi-MB bundle/artifact downloads over slow edge links after backend FIN; drain timeout must reset on write progress up to a hard cap while preserving 5000ms for stalled-idle sockets. In the auto-updater, b072e6a lacks a light-node mesh artifact-get/chunk-pull wire protocol over validator DataChannels, leaves :fetch-pointer HTTPS-only, never dispatches :blob-fetch-failed back into reduce-light-update-tick, and lacks a :phase == :watching guard on :poll-tick/:pointer-seen—causing an infinite generation-bump download livelock whenever poll-interval-ms < download duration (e.g. 15s mesh + 30s HTTPS fallback > 5-30s poll). Moreover, :restore-lkg-light-bundle only deletes .tmp instead of restoring .lkg and persisting failed-CID quarantine across boot-loops. For Open Question 3: reject a flat 15000ms wall-clock timeout in favor of a 5000-10000ms stall/first-byte timeout with size-scaled total ceiling and :phase-guarded polling.",
