   :verdict :objection,
   :critique
   "From the Founder / Human Steward perspective anchored in Bitecho Shared Values v0.1, BIP-021 cannot be consented as written due to non-negotiable AEAD safety flaws, silent user-agency/sovereignty violations, metadata privacy leakage, and under-specified coupling across six pillars.\n\n1. Individual Agency, Consent & YubiKey/Browser UX (Shared Values v0.1): §2.5.3 silently auto-uploads Rolodex snapshots on every petname or subnet edit ('debounced rolodex-put') and auto-merges remote snapshots on YubiKey unlock without explicit user opt-in. Worse, exec-artifact-put defaults burn to 100 ECH when economy-enabled? is true — silently draining user ECH per UI edit or failing outright for 0-ECH browser users. Naive map merging without deletion tombstones silently resurrects deleted petnames and unsubscribed subnets, conflicts with Hive's separate localStorage hive_subscribed_subnets, and crashes in webcrypto-non-extractable mode where raw private key bytes do not exist in JS memory. Similarly, §2.5.1 masks degraded WebRTC state by setting connected: true and transport_healthy: true (suppressing :connection-error) whenever relay fallback is healthy, violating Hive's DEGRADED (relay) honesty contract, and misses browser-cli/dispatch-browser-cli + get-status used by window.BitechoLightNode.\n\n2. Cryptographic Hygiene & Non-Negotiable Safety: (a) §2.4 proposes advancing :tx-seq to peer-high-seq after a :replayed-seq session-ack and recommending 'p2p-offer' for key rotation. That premise is broken twice: first, exec-p2p-offer only renegotiates ICE/UDP candidates and NEVER rotates X25519/AEAD session keys (:tx-key/:rx-key); second, by the time the receiver returns :replayed-seq, the rolled-back sender has ALREADY encrypted and transmitted a second distinct plaintext under the same (:tx-key, seq->nonce(k)), breaking two-time-pad confidentiality and leaking the GCM GHASH H or Poly1305 (r,s) key. Continuing to send seq = peer-high-seq + 1 on that tainted :tx-key is unsafe. Furthermore, rollbacks > 64 messages hit :stale-seq in sealed/check-and-advance-replay-window, which handle-wire-sealed-msg drops with zero session-ack and zero diagnostic — leaving large VM-snapshot rollbacks permanently blackholed. (b) §2.5.3 omits AEAD cipher, AAD, and nonce derivation rules; deterministic seq-based nonces across IndexedDB wipes, multi-tab edits, or --force restores cause catastrophic (key, nonce) reuse, and 'bitecho/rolodex-v1' collides awkwardly with BIP-016's 'bitecho/rolodex/v1' while bypassing BIP-016 bounds and revocations. Crucially, publishing rolodex/<pubkey-hex>/latest.enc with cleartext :pubkey and pointer/authority on unauthenticated /artifact/v1/list leaks the entire user identity roster, exact YubiKey-unlock/edit timestamps, and unpadded contact-count size deltas. (c) §2.2's subnet-admitted? returns true for nil and '*', allowing any burned direct envelope (outer '*', inner nil) to bypass subscribed-subnets checks.\n\n3. Scope Discipline & Operational Simplicity: Bundling four tight bugfixes (§2.1, §2.2, §2.3, §2.6.1) with two under-specified crypto/protocol/UI designs (§2.4 rekeying and §2.5.3 on-network Rolodex + Hive parity) risks delaying urgent hardening or shipping weak cryptography. Additionally: §2.1 must fail closed if reconcile-startup-subnet-id crosses canonical :network-uri boundaries (devnet/testnet/mainnet state bleed); §2.3/§2.4 emit event names missing from actionable-event-types (silently dropped from :event-queue / ctl wait) and allow unauthenticated broadcast-sig-invalid floods to evict the 256-entry :event-queue; §2.6.1 needs a global :draining connection cap on 1 GB e2-micro nodes and explicit :cancel-drain-timeout; and §2.6.2 hits a generation livelock in deploy/core.cljc/reduce-light-update-tick when --auto-update-interval-ms < 45s while lacking chunked mesh blob fetch for 1.6 MiB+ bundles.\n\n4. Explicit Answers to §4 Open Questions: OQ1 (§2.4): Freeze outbound sends (fail-closed) on rollback — advance :tx-seq to peer-high-seq so lower seqs never re-emit, latch :tx-tainted? true, emit counted diagnostics, and require an explicit X25519-rekeying offer/accept or rendezvous-offer (NOT p2p-offer, which never rekeys) before resuming sends (preserving explicit human consent when autonomous? is false). OQ2 (§2.5): Reject a single global public bitecho.org/rolodex roster as specified; after fixing consent, padding, and ACLs, store encrypted Rolodex pointers under the user's configured home/primary subnet (or opt-in backup subnet) using a blinded HKDF path tag and unlinkable HKDF-derived signing subkey so /artifact/v1/list cannot correlate pointers to the user's main pubkey or activity timestamps. OQ3 (§2.6): Reject a flat 15,000 ms timeout; enforce a two-tier budget — short connect/first-byte stall timeout (min(5000 ms, max(2000 ms, 4 * peer_rtt))) plus a progress-checked size-scaled ceiling (clamp(15000 ms, 60000 ms, size_bytes / min_bps)) over BIP-017a chunked mesh fetch — combined with a :fetching/:health-checking phase guard in reduce-light-update-tick so :poll-tick never bumps :generation mid-download.",
